# Fleet-level cron schedule — seven cross-cutting jobs moved off jesse's host
# crontab (see ../HANDOFF.md and README.md). Bind-mounted live into the
# fleet-cron container (NOT baked into the image): edit this file and
# `docker compose restart cron` — no rebuild needed. This is the amputeenews
# variant of the site cron pattern, chosen deliberately because the baked-in
# version has already cost the fleet unscheduled jobs elsewhere
# (project_cron_image_drift_2026-08-10).
#
# Schedules are unchanged from the host crontab entries they replace — do not
# retune cadences here without a separate, deliberate change.
#
# Job 7 (registry-drift-cron.sh) landed on the host crontab the same day this
# tool was built (fleet-registry work), after HANDOFF.md's six-job brief was
# already written — folded in here 2026-08-15 as a same-day follow-up, not
# part of the original migration commit.
# --- Job 1: fleet cron self-heal (Option A, see ../HANDOFF.md §4) ---
# This is the job that heals dead SITE scheduler containers. The healer
# healing itself would be circular, so the ONE thing left on the host is
# ensure-up.sh via a single @reboot line — see its header for the full
# reasoning. Do not add a second watchdog container to watch this one.
*/10 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/ensure-fleet-cron.sh

# --- Job 2: SecurityScanner temp-scans purge (docker-socket only) ---
15 4 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/purge-secscan-temp-scans.sh

# --- Job 3: fleet-wide stuck-worker reaper ---
7,22,37,52 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/reap-stuck-workers-fleet.sh

# --- Job 4: fleet Claude Code auth health watchdog ---
*/15 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/check-claude-auth.sh

# --- Job 5: fleet lint sweep ---
20 6 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/lint-sweep-cron.sh

# --- Job 6: Fleet Dashboard domain-jobs spool drainer ---
* * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/domain-job-runner.sh

# --- Job 7: fleet registry drift check (zero-AI, filesystem + YAML only) ---
35 6 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/registry-drift-cron.sh

# --- Job 8: domain-developer worker Claude-auth guard ---
# 2026-08-16 incident: idle dd-* workers drifted past the host's OAuth
# rotation and went to "Not logged in". 2026-08-23 incident: worse than
# that — a stale worker credential got REDEEMED (not just expired), and
# refresh-token reuse-detection revoked the whole shared family, logging out
# the real host session too. Fixed by pushing a fresh copy of the host
# credential into every worker EVERY TICK (cheap file copy, no API call —
# see sync_credentials() in the script) instead of only at container start/
# restart. Cadence tightened 30min -> 10min on 2026-08-23 so that sync window
# — the actual defense now — stays tight. Later the same day the workers
# became cattle (job 9), which BOUNDS a worker's unattended lifetime instead
# of merely patching it every tick, so the script's proactive-restart loop
# was deleted rather than retuned and self-heal now destroys instead of
# restarting. See script header for the full incident writeup — companion to
# job 4, which only checks the host session.
*/10 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/check-dd-containers-auth.sh

# --- Job 9: idle dd-* worker reaper (cattle, not pets) ---
# domain-developer workers are meant to be spun up "on demand" (README.md)
# but `--restart unless-stopped` with no idle-timeout meant once started they
# ran indefinitely — the root enabler of both auth incidents above (a worker
# that's been up for days is a worker whose credential copy has had days to
# drift). State lives entirely on host bind mounts (REDESIGN.md), so a
# destroyed worker loses nothing and `domain-developer <site>` / the panel
# brings it back in seconds, re-running the entrypoint's fresh credential
# copy on the way up.
#
# This job is only ONE THIRD of the cattle fix and cannot deliver it alone —
# the create path (bin/domain-developer + server/server.js: no restart
# policy, destroy-and-recreate instead of `docker start`) and the verifier
# (bin/dd-doctor's CATTLE checks) are the other two. Read the script header
# before changing any of them.
#
# Stops AND REMOVES any dd-* worker that is idle past
# DD_IDLE_THRESHOLD_SEC (default 4h), is running a stale image while idle
# past DD_DRIFT_IDLE_GRACE_SEC (15min), or is a dead container object past
# DD_CORPSE_GRACE_SEC (1h, kept that long so a crash-on-boot is still
# inspectable with `docker logs`). Removing rather than stopping is load-
# bearing: a stopped container pins the image it was created from forever.
# Supports --dry-run.
*/30 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/reap-idle-dd-workers.sh

# --- Job 10: fleet Docker garbage collection ---
# Nothing ever collected Docker build exhaust. Measured 2026-08-23 before the
# first run: 587 images / 241 GB (139 GB reclaimable), 159 dangling images, and
# a 158 GB build cache — on a root filesystem at 71%. First run reclaimed
# 73.3 GB and took the disk to 67%.
#
# Two entries on purpose:
#   nightly  — dangling images + build cache not used in 168h. Cheap, keeps a
#              warm cache so the next image bump is fast.
#   weekly   — --cache-all, which drops the until= filter. Needed because
#              until= matches on LAST USED and much of this host's cache has
#              none, so the nightly pass alone silently reclaims nothing (the
#              script cross-checks and warns when that happens — see its
#              header). The weekly pass is what actually reclaims; the cost is
#              one cold build afterwards.
#
# NEVER touches volumes. See the script header and REDESIGN.md's 2026-05-30
# incident for why volume deletion only goes through a verified-delete path.
40 3 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/gc-docker.sh
10 3 * * 0 /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/gc-docker.sh --cache-all

# --- Job 11: fleet shared-image drift watchdog ---
# Asserts the fleet-images invariants continuously: every site on the current
# fleet-site-cron image, crontab bind-mounted with a source file that actually
# exists, container running as uid 1000, scheduler holding a non-zero job
# count. Healthy = SILENT; alerts to Slack on failure with a 6h cooldown.
#
# This is the half of the consolidation that makes it stick. The pre-commit
# guard blocks NEW per-site Dockerfiles; this catches the rest — a container
# left on a stale image after an image bump, a hand-edited compose, a
# scheduler that came up loading zero jobs. Without it the fleet drifts back
# to 53 divergent definitions the same way it got there the first time.
#
# Does NOT use --probe-worker (a container start per site) — that is a rollout
# gate, run by hand after an image roll, not a background job.
50 */6 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/fleet-images-drift-cron.sh

# --- Job 12: shared-image base-digest drift check ---
# The two shared images pin their bases by DIGEST, which buys reproducibility
# and costs free security updates on rebuild. That trade is only honest if
# someone notices when the upstream tag moves — otherwise the pin quietly rots
# and the next person rips it out to get unstuck.
#
# Weekly, read-only: compares each pinned digest against what the tag resolves
# to now and exits non-zero when a bump is available. Does NOT build, tag or
# roll anything. Bump deliberately with:
#     tools/fleet-images/bin/fleet-image-bases --update
#     tools/fleet-images/bin/fleet-image-build all --version <next>
# fleet-image-smoke gates the promotion, so a bad base cannot reach :latest.
30 5 * * 1 /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/fleet-images/bin/fleet-image-bases --check


# --- Job 13: fleet tool-test sweep FRESHNESS watchdog ---
# The sweep itself (tools/scripts/fleet-test-cron.sh) is the ONE fleet job that
# cannot live in this container. It runs 25 tool test suites whose dependency
# toolchain — pytest, fastapi, httpx, respx, playwright, PIL, atproto, the
# google client stack — exists only in the host's pyenv 3.11.10. That
# interpreter is glibc-2.38-linked and will not load in this Alpine/musl
# container, nor in fleet-site-worker (Debian bookworm, glibc 2.36 — verified,
# it errors on libm GLIBC_2.38). Reproducing the toolchain in a pinned image is
# the right long-term answer and is tracked as follow-up work; until then the
# sweep runs from the host crontab, where the toolchain actually is.
#
# Host cron's failure mode is that a wiped crontab is SILENT — the exact thing
# the fleet-cron migration existed to kill. This watchdog is what buys that
# back: it asserts a sweep report landed in the last 26h and Slacks if not. So
# the host runs it, and the fleet notices when the host stops.
30 8 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/fleet-test-freshness.sh

# --- Job 14: fleet git-hygiene sweep ---
# Classifies every dirty path in the monorepo + all sites/* submodules against
# tools/fleet-git/policy.json, then commits / ignores / pushes what policy
# recognises and queues the rest on the Fleet Dashboard's Git Hygiene tab.
#
# This is the standing fix for the drift class that keeps recurring: a cron
# role writes files, "succeeds", and never pushes — so the site is silently
# stale, and the parent repo's submodule pointer never moves either. The sweep
# also bumps those pointers, but ONLY for a site it left clean AND pushed.
#
# Safety lives in the tool, not here: never `git add -A`; a credential-shaped
# path halts that repo entirely; a repo behind upstream or on a detached HEAD
# is reported, never acted on; an unrecognised path is queued, never guessed.
#
# Healthy = SILENT, same contract as the lint/test sweeps. Slack fires only on
# a blocked credential, a git error, or a review item unresolved for >24h.
# Re-enabled 2026-08-25 after the adversarial review's four criticals were
# fixed and covered by tests (42 total, incl. a real parent+submodule fixture
# for the pointer path): deletions now need per-rule opt-in and stay under a
# cap; a pointer bump requires the submodule HEAD to be provably on its remote
# and pins the verified SHA; mechanical commits build a scratch index so a
# site's own cron cannot have its work swept in; operator globs cannot be
# universal and the untrack list is capped.
17 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/fleet-git-hygiene-cron.sh

# --- Job 15: cron-freshness sweep (tier 2) ---
# Asserts that every site's supercronic is still FIRING, not merely alive.
#
# Tier 1 is the per-container Docker healthcheck each site now carries
# (`grep -qx supercronic /proc/1/comm`, 120s, + `labels: autoheal=true`), which
# catches a DEAD scheduler and lets vpn-autoheal restart it unattended. It is
# deliberately trivial — ~8ms CPU, no shell, no network, no docker CLI — and so
# it cannot see a supercronic that is alive but wedged: a job that never
# returned, a lock held forever, a crontab that parsed to zero jobs. That is
# the 2026-05-17 failure mode, schedulers silently dead for 9 days.
#
# Seeing THAT needs schedule reasoning plus log reads. Doing it per container
# would be 26 heavyweight probes on a loop — the same mistake as autoheal's
# baked-in 5s HEALTHCHECK firing a runc exec every 5s. So it runs here instead:
# one host-side process for the whole fleet, ~2.6s wall, every 30 min. The
# window is derived per site from that site's own crontab (longest legitimate
# gap x2 + 15m grace), never hardcoded — a weekly-only site must not be called
# stale on Tuesday.
#
# Healthy = SILENT, same contract as the lint/git/image sweeps.
23,53 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/cron-freshness-cron.sh

# --- Job 16: daily AI-cost analyst (ai-optimizer) ---
# Answers the standing "what can we improve about our AI usage?" question once
# a day instead of from scratch each morning. analyst.py builds the evidence
# packet with ZERO AI (ledger + a git before/after check per candidate role,
# which auto-excludes roles someone already fixed mid-window); a claude -p
# session runs only if there are live candidates, and only ever FILES tickets
# — it never changes code. Healthy is SILENT: Slack fires only when a new
# ticket was actually filed.
# Review at Fleet Dashboard -> Growth -> AI Optimizer.
# 06:45 ET — after the lint sweep (:20) and registry drift (:35), so the
# morning batch stays sequential rather than three heavy jobs at once.
# Kill switch: touch tools/ai-optimizer/.analyst-disabled
45 6 * * *    /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/ai-optimizer-cron.sh

# --- Job 17: AI-cost fix implementer (ai-optimizer) ---
# Applies ONE human-approved ticket per run, to ONE canary site, then files a
# SEPARATE fan-out ticket that needs its own approval before any other site is
# touched (two human yeses for anything fleet-wide — see
# project_ai_optimizer_queue / feedback_no_auto_rollout_tool). risk:high
# tickets are never auto-applied. Exits in milliseconds at zero cost when
# nothing is approved, which is most ticks — the frequent cadence only exists
# so an approval lands within ~20 min instead of waiting for tomorrow.
# Kill switch: touch tools/ai-optimizer/.implement-disabled
# Dry run:     AI_IMPL_DRY_RUN=1 tools/scripts/ai-optimizer-implement.sh
11,31,51 * * * *   /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/ai-optimizer-implement.sh

# --- Job 18: Social Hub pipeline tick ---
# The supervised API container owns dependencies and live state; fleet-cron
# owns the versioned quarter-hour schedule and invokes the idempotent tick in it.
0,15,30,45 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/social-hub/run-tick.sh

