8,23,38,53 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/social-controller/run.sh
13,28,43,58 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/social-controller/monitor.py
12 4 * * * docker exec social-hub-api python3 -m social_hub.cli maintain

# --- Job 16: host-watchdog assertion (the other half of job 15) ---
# Asserts the HOST-side fleet-cron watchdog is still running.
#
# Job 15 covers all 26 site schedulers, but it cannot cover THIS container: a
# wedged fleet-cron is precisely what would fail to run the sweep that would
# have reported it. So fleet-cron-freshness-host.sh runs from the host crontab
# every 15 min. Host cron's failure mode is that a wiped crontab is SILENT —
# the exact thing the fleet-cron migration existed to kill — so this job
# asserts that host check's report landed in the last hour.
#
# Each side watches the other; neither is trusted to notice its own absence.
# Same shape as job 13 (fleet-test-freshness.sh) — if you change one, look at
# the other.
#
# Healthy = SILENT.
41 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/fleet-cron-host-watchdog.sh

# --- Job 14: env-broker credential-policy drift check ---
# Site containers no longer mount the fleet .env. Each gets a rendered file
# holding only the keys its ops/ actually references (~4, vs the 60-key shared
# file that included GITHUB_TOKEN, the Amazon secret, the PIA password and
# FD_TOKEN — the Fleet Dashboard operator token — handed to containers that run
# `claude -p` over scraped, attacker-influenced input).
#
# Two ways that quietly breaks: a role starts using a key the policy does not
# grant (fails at runtime hours later, looking like anything but a credential
# problem), or a key stays granted after the script needing it is gone (blast
# radius nobody is paying for). This re-derives usage from the ops trees and
# diffs it against the allowlist, and checks each rendered file still exists
# and is 0400.
#
# DETECTION ONLY — never re-renders. Rollouts stay deliberate
# (feedback_no_auto_rollout_tool). Healthy = SILENT, 24h alert cooldown.
25 7 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/env-broker-check-cron.sh

# --- Job 18: fleet local-data retention sweep ---
# Nothing on this host had a retention step of any kind. Measured 2026-08-25:
# tools/cf-stats/out at 423 MB across 112 daily JSONL files growing ~5 MB/day,
# and sites/*/ops/logs at 74,185 files, on a root filesystem at 70%.
#
# It COMPRESSES, it does not delete. cf-stats is the only historical
# Cloudflare record on this host and nothing backs it up; the site role logs
# are the audit trail for autonomous publishing runs and get read during
# incidents. Ledgers older than 30d are gzipped in place; site .log/.txt/.png
# older than 30d roll into one tar.gz per site per DAY — day-bucketing so every
# file sharing an mtime date ages out on the same run and each bucket is
# written exactly once, which is what makes a daily job idempotent with no
# archive-merging problem. First run: 135 ledgers + 19,781 log files, 247 MB
# reclaimed, 26s, zero errors.
#
# The *.jsonl under ops/logs are deliberately OUT of scope — they are ledgers,
# not logs (tools/ai-usage/aggregate.py globs token-usage-*.jsonl,
# tools/engineer-fleet/engineer-status.py reads engineer-heartbeat-*.jsonl).
# Archiving them would silently truncate fleet AI-cost history for 8 MB.
#
# Paired with tools/cf-grafana/ingest.py, which now globs .jsonl.gz as well as
# .jsonl. Those two must stay in step: compressing under an ingester that only
# sees plain .jsonl drops fleet history from Grafana without erroring anywhere.
# Verified by ingesting to a scratch DB before and after the first run — all
# eight tables identical, 2,548 snapshots either way.
#
# Every compression is verified before the original goes: the .gz is
# decompressed and sha256-compared, the tar reopened and its members counted.
# A failure leaves the original in place and retries next run. Supports
# --dry-run. Healthy = SILENT; Slacks only on errors or a >=500 MB reclaim.
45 4 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/prune-fleet-data-cron.sh

# --- Job 15: host-wide container watchdog ---
# The Fleet Dashboard's Containers tab scopes to compose projects inside the
# domains checkout. That is correct for the tab and wrong for a watchdog: an
# Exited(255) container from another project sat dead for six days with nothing
# reporting it (B11), because nobody owned it. This scans EVERY container on the
# host for four states that all look fine from a distance — dead-nonzero,
# stopped-despite-restart-policy, crashlooping, and failing its own healthcheck.
#
# Healthy = SILENT. It alerts only when the finding set CHANGES, so a container
# unhealthy for a week does not re-alert hourly until the alert means nothing.
# It DOES always alert if it cannot query docker at all — a blind watchdog
# reporting "all clear" is worse than no watchdog.
23 * * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/container-watch-cron.sh

# --- Job 19: fleet offsite backup ---
# Git covers the code; nothing covered the STATE. cf-stats' JSONL is the only
# historical Cloudflare record anywhere, data-hub and site-tracker hold
# databases (data-hub's lives in a named Docker volume, so a filesystem-only
# backup silently skips it), Gatus holds the uptime history, and the dashboard
# holds the only record of which agent pushed or restarted what. A host loss
# erased all of it — tools/credential-vault-backup covers Vaultwarden alone.
#
# Uploads to R2, verifies each object by length + sha256 before recording the
# run, prunes past 30 days. Secrets ABORT the run rather than being filtered:
# an offsite copy of the fleet's credentials is worse than no backup at all.
# Incremental for the append-only groups.
#
# Runs at 03:20, before the 03:40 docker GC and well clear of the 04:45
# retention sweep that prunes the very files this is backing up.
# Like the tool-test sweep (Job 13), the backup itself cannot run in this
# container: it needs boto3, which is not installed here and would have to be
# baked into an Alpine image to be. It runs from the host crontab where the
# toolchain is, and THIS job is the freshness watchdog that makes a wiped host
# crontab loud instead of silent — the failure mode the fleet-cron migration
# existed to kill. A backup that stopped running and told nobody is not a
# backup.
40 8 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/fleet-backup-freshness.sh

# --- Job 20: domain renewal collection ---
# Renewals are the portfolio's largest recurring hard cost and nothing tracked
# them until 2026-09-01. Pulls expiry + auto_renew from Cloudflare Registrar
# into tools/registrar/cache/latest.json, which the Fleet Dashboard's
# parked-inventory panel and /api/registrar read — so the panel never makes a
# live Cloudflare call to render a tab.
#
# Runs in THIS container (unlike jobs 13/19): it needs only python3 stdlib, the
# repo mount and outbound HTTPS, all of which are here.
#
# Healthy = SILENT. Alerts only when a domain has auto-renew OFF and a renewal
# inside 90 days — a date on its own is not news, a date with nothing set to
# renew it is — or when collection fails, which would otherwise leave the
# dashboard serving silently ageing dates.
#
# 05:40: clear of the 03:20 backup, 03:40 docker GC and 04:45 retention sweep.
40 5 * * * /home/jesse/projects/domains/tools/scripts/registrar-cron.sh
